<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n1256" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Aeŏlĭa</span></b> , ae,
			f., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Αἰολία</span>. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A group of islands near Sicily</i>, <i>so called after Æolus</i>, <i>who is
				said to have once reigned there</i>, now <i>the
				Lipari Islands</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 3, 8, 14, § 92
			sq.</span></i>— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			In mythol., <i>the abode of Æolus</i>, <i>the god of the winds.</i>
			<i><span class="en">Verg. A. 1,
				52</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>III.</b>
			<i>A country of Asia Minor</i>, <i><span class="en">Nep. Con.
			5</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
